# The one fixture for the cohort gavel's cycles, and what it pins is two nodes
# named by hand.
#
# The gavel of 2026-08-29 -- "block-born is the whole cohort" -- asked for four
# shapes, and the build-hole gavel of 2026-09-16 took two of them back: a
# record an `if` chose and an element of a born list can no longer be written
# through, because a hole is filled exactly once and a name whose birth is
# `Either` cannot be shown to fill one. The alias and the field of a born node
# stay, and they are what this file exercises.
#
# WHAT IT DOES NOT PIN, measured 2026-09-18 and recorded in the log under
# "birth through a call, measured": the gavel's own sentence asks for cyclic
# structures SIZED BY DATA, and no program can write one. A hole is refused
# outside a `build` block, so the maker cannot be a function; it cannot escape
# the block it was written in, so the maker cannot be a `build` either; a
# lambda inside the block is outside it for that rule, which closes the `map`
# shape; and a fill's target parses as a bare name, so N nodes need N names. A
# value a call returns is also not block-born, which is a separate refusal and
# the only one that widening `born_of` would lift.
#
# So two names is not a choice this fixture made. It is the largest cycle the
# language admits, and what it would take to admit more is a question in
# design/pending-gavels.md rather than a build anybody can start.
type node
  id
  peers

pub play =
  build
    a = node "a" _
    b = node "b" [a]
    a.peers = [b]
  graph = [a b]
  build
    base = node "seed" _
    base.peers = [base]
  print "{graph}"
  >> print "{base}"
